@inherits RazorSlice<StyleGuideViewModel>
@using Elastic.Documentation.Site.StyleGuide
@implements IUsesLayout<Elastic.Documentation.Site.StyleGuide._StyleGuideLayout, StyleGuideViewModel>
@functions {
	public StyleGuideViewModel LayoutModel => Model;
}
@{
	var devices = new[]
	{
		(Title: "Contained, 1440px window", Width: 1440, Scale: "0.34", Full: false),
		(Title: "Contained, 2560px window", Width: 2560, Scale: "0.19", Full: false),
		(Title: "Full width, 1440px window", Width: 1440, Scale: "0.34", Full: true),
		(Title: "Full width, 2560px window", Width: 2560, Scale: "0.19", Full: true)
	};
}
<section class="sg-section">
	<h2>Two width modes</h2>
	<p><strong>Contained</strong> is the default. The page box is as wide as the elastic.co header (<code>--layout-max</code>), centred, and the header follows the token, so the logo, the docs bar, the sidebar text and the content start on one line. The assembler has to be contained, because the header is not ours.</p>
	<p><strong>Full width</strong> (<code>layout--full</code>) lets the sidebar anchor to the left of the window, with the content and rail centred in what is left. It still stops at <code>--layout-max-wide</code> and centres beyond it, so a 4K window is not sparse. Each window below is drawn to scale: the bar on top is the header, the dashed boxes are the sidebar, content and rail.</p>
	<div class="grid grid--2 sg-devices">
		@foreach (var device in devices)
		{
			<figure class="sg-device" style="--w: @(device.Width)px; --h: @(device.Width * 3 / 10)px; --sg-scale: @device.Scale">
				<figcaption>@device.Title</figcaption>
				<div class="sg-device__frame">
				<div class="sg-device__screen">
					<div class="sg-device__header"><div class="layout layout--single"><div class="sg-slot">Header · --layout-max</div></div></div>
					<div class="layout layout--three@(device.Full ? " layout--full" : "")">
						<aside class="layout__nav"><div class="sg-slot sg-slot--nav">Sidebar</div></aside>
						<div class="layout__main">
							<main class="layout__content"><div class="sg-slot sg-slot--content">Content</div></main>
							<aside class="layout__rail"><div class="sg-slot sg-slot--rail">Rail</div></aside>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>
				</div>
			</figure>
		}
	</div>
</section>
<section class="sg-section">
	<h2>Content measure, sidebar and rail</h2>
	<p>The content is optimised for <code>--layout-measure</code>, 90 characters a line (110 in full width). The sidebar and rail fall in line from that: whatever is left of the box after the content and the gaps is shared between them, 55% to the sidebar and 45% to the rail, and each stops growing at its maximum. Beyond that the content grows rather than the sidebar and rail, or in full width the whole group is centred. Below its minimum a column stops shrinking and the content gives way; the rail drops at 1100px and the sidebar becomes a drawer at 860px.</p>
	<table class="sg-table">
		<thead><tr><th>Token</th><th>Value and use</th></tr></thead>
		<tbody>
		@foreach (var token in StyleGuideTokens.Layouts)
		{
			<tr>
				<td><code>@token.Name</code></td>
				<td>@token.Role</td>
			</tr>
		}
		</tbody>
	</table>
</section>
<section class="sg-section">
	<h2>Units</h2>
	<p>Layout sizes are in <code>rem</code>, and the reading measure is in <code>ch</code>. Nothing is in <code>px</code> but hairlines and the breakpoints.</p>
	<table class="sg-table">
		<thead><tr><th>Unit</th><th>Used for</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
		<tbody>
		<tr><td><code>rem</code></td><td>Page widths, gutters, gaps, sidebar and rail widths, spacing, type</td><td>Follows the reader's font-size setting and browser zoom, so the layout scales with the text instead of cramping it. One root size means every number reads the same.</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>ch</code></td><td>The content measure only</td><td>The width of a "0" in the content's own font, so 90 means 90 characters whatever the font or size. Layout sizes are not in <code>ch</code>: they would shift when a font falls back.</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>%</code> and <code>fr</code></td><td>Sharing out what is left</td><td>The sidebar and rail are a share of the free space, clamped between rem limits.</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>em</code></td><td>Not used for layout</td><td>It compounds with nesting, so the same value means different things in different places.</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>px</code></td><td>Borders and the three breakpoints</td><td>Media queries cannot read custom properties, and a hairline should stay a hairline.</td></tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>
</section>
<section class="sg-section">
	<h2>Single column</h2>
	<p>No sidebar: the content takes the page width. Landing pages, archives, the search page and the 404 are single-column. Add <code>layout--narrow</code> for text-led pages that read better at <code>--layout-narrow-max</code>.</p>
	<div class="sg-frame">
		<div class="layout layout--single">
			<div class="layout__main"><div class="sg-slot sg-slot--content">Content</div></div>
		</div>
	</div>
	<pre class="sg-themed"><code>&lt;div class="layout layout--single"&gt;
  &lt;div class="layout__main"&gt;
    &lt;main class="layout__content"&gt;…&lt;/main&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</section>
<section class="sg-section">
	<h2>Hub</h2>
	<p>A single column that ignores the page width: the hero band and the sections run edge to edge over a wash, and bring their own inner width. Hub pages use <code>layout--single layout--hub</code>.</p>
	<pre class="sg-themed"><code>&lt;div class="layout layout--single layout--hub"&gt;
  &lt;div class="layout__main"&gt;
    &lt;main class="layout__content"&gt;hero, sections…&lt;/main&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</section>
<section class="sg-section">
	<h2>Two columns</h2>
	<p>Sidebar and content. The API pages use it: the content has no rail because the operation view opens its own examples column. Add <code>layout--full</code> for the full-width mode.</p>
	<pre class="sg-themed"><code>&lt;div class="layout layout--two"&gt;
  &lt;aside class="layout__nav"&gt;…&lt;/aside&gt;
  &lt;div class="layout__main"&gt;
    &lt;main class="layout__content"&gt;…&lt;/main&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</section>
<section class="sg-section">
	<h2>Three columns</h2>
	<p>Sidebar, content and a right-hand rail for the outline, page actions and feedback. Documentation articles use it. At 1100px the rail drops above the content as a compact row.</p>
	<pre class="sg-themed"><code>&lt;div class="layout layout--three"&gt;
  &lt;aside class="layout__nav"&gt;…&lt;/aside&gt;
  &lt;div class="layout__main"&gt;
    &lt;main class="layout__content"&gt;…&lt;/main&gt;
    &lt;aside class="layout__rail"&gt;…&lt;/aside&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</section>
<section class="sg-section">
	<h2>Breakpoints</h2>
	<p>Custom properties cannot be used in media queries, so these numbers are written out wherever they apply. They are the only three the system uses.</p>
	<table class="sg-table">
		<thead><tr><th>Width</th><th>What changes</th></tr></thead>
		<tbody>
		<tr><td><code>1100px</code></td><td>Three columns become two: the rail moves above the content and drops its outline and call to action. Four-column grids become two.</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>860px</code></td><td>The sidebar becomes a drawer opened from a Menu button, so every layout is a single column.</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>760px</code></td><td>Content grids become a single column.</td></tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>
</section>
<section class="sg-section">
	<h2>Starting a new page</h2>
	<ol>
		<li>Pick the layout: <code>layout--single</code> without a sidebar, <code>layout--two</code> with one, <code>layout--three</code> with a sidebar and a rail. Add <code>layout--full</code> only where the page wants the whole window.</li>
		<li>Put a full-width band (hero, banner) outside the layout, and its content inside a <code>layout layout--single</code>, so it lines up with the page below.</li>
		<li>Do not set widths, gutters or breakpoints in a page's own CSS; if the layouts do not fit, that is a gap to bring back here. Spacing and grids are on <a href="@StyleGuideCatalog.Route/grid">Grid and rhythm</a>.</li>
	</ol>
</section>
